WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:10.720
In August 1347, a decade into the Hundred Years&nbsp;
War, the port city of Calais surrendered to King&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:18.160
Edward III of England after an eleven-month siege.&nbsp;
As the mayor of Calais and five of his fellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:25.440
townsmen surrendered the city’s keys to Edward,&nbsp;
the King ordered their execution as punishment for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:33.200
their refusal to submit. While Edward refused the&nbsp;
pleas of close friends to spare them, his pregnant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:40.160
queen Philippa of Hainault sank to her knees.&nbsp;
She begged of her husband, “that for the Son of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:47.680
the Holy Mary and for the love of me, you shall&nbsp;
wish to have mercy on these six men”. Edward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:55.200
one of England’s greatest warrior kings, was moved&nbsp;
by his wife’s intervention and relented. The tale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:02.000
of the townsmen or burghers of Calais is regarded&nbsp;
as a vivid demonstration of the power of medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:09.440
queenship. However, Philippa’s power and influence&nbsp;
as Queen of England was not confined to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:16.880
single spectacle. She effectively deputised for&nbsp;
Edward as a political and military leader while he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:23.840
was away on campaign, and her dynastic connections&nbsp;
on the continent made her a useful diplomat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:31.120
Philippa and Edward were happily married and she&nbsp;
performed her dynastic role effectively by having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:37.440
several surviving sons to secure the future&nbsp;
of the House of Plantagenet. Nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.520
Philippa’s childbearing prowess&nbsp;
would also set the stage for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.480
bitter internecine struggles which would&nbsp;
divide the English realm in the century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:53.280
after her death. This is the story of&nbsp;
Philippa of Hainault, Queen of England.
&nbsp;

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:13.200
The woman known to history as Philippa of Hainault&nbsp;
was born in Valenciennes in the County of Hainaut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:19.440
on the western borderlands of the Holy Roman&nbsp;
Empire. While the historical county gives its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:25.280
name to the modern-day Belgian province of&nbsp;
Hainaut, Philippa’s hometown of Valenciennes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:34.960
has been part of France since the 17th century.&nbsp;
While her accepted date of birth is 24 June 1314,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:43.040
this is unlikely. The Queen was “almost fourteen&nbsp;
years old” at her wedding in January 1328,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:51.600
according to the chronicler Jean Froissart who&nbsp;
was patronised by Philippa in the 1360s. In 1319,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:57.760
King Edward II of England dispatched the Bishop&nbsp;
of Exeter, Walter Stapledon, to the court&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:03.200
at Hainaut to consider the suitability of the&nbsp;
Count’s daughters as a bride for Prince Edward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:08.800
the future Edward III. He left a detailed&nbsp;
account of one of the Count’s daughters who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:14.800
would be “nine years on St John’s day next&nbsp;
to come”. This would place Philippa’s date&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:22.400
of birth on 24 June 1310 or 1311. While this&nbsp;
account was later understood to have been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:28.560
description of Philippa as she later married&nbsp;
Edward, modern scholarship indicates that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:36.240
Stapledon was referring to Philippa’s elder sister&nbsp;
Margarethe. On the basis of Froissart’s account,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:45.600
Philippa’s biographer Kathryn Warner argues that&nbsp;
she was born between January and March 1314.
&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:54.640
Philippa’s father Count William I of Hainault was&nbsp;
a member of the House of Avesnes, whose ancestral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.640 --> 00:04:01.440
estates were in the village of Avesnes-sur-Helpe&nbsp;
in northern France. In addition to counties of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:07.840
Hainault and Avesnes, William also ruled over the&nbsp;
counties of Holland and Zeeland and spent much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:14.720
of his life at war with the Count of Flanders. In&nbsp;
1305 he married Joan of Valois, the granddaughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:22.320
of King Philip III of France. When the male line&nbsp;
of the House of Capet was extinguished in 1328,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:29.120
Joan’s brother became King Philip VI of France,&nbsp;
asserting his claims over those of his distant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:35.760
cousin Edward III of England. The rival claims&nbsp;
of Philippa’s uncle and husband to the kingdom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:44.720
of France would trigger an Anglo-French conflict&nbsp;
which would last for more than a century.
&nbsp;

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:51.520
Philippa grew up in a large family with eight or&nbsp;
nine full siblings, of whom around half survived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:58.000
into adulthood. Her father Count William also&nbsp;
had at least eight illegitimate children whom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:04.240
he acknowledged as his own and may have brought&nbsp;
up in his household. Count William ruled over a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:09.920
collection of small but wealthy counties in the&nbsp;
Low Countries who owed their prosperity to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:16.400
commercial relationship with England across the&nbsp;
North Sea. It was no surprise that King Edward II&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:23.440
of England had sought the hand of one of William’s&nbsp;
daughters for his son and heir in the late 1310s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:30.480
Bishop Stapelton’s detailed account of William’s&nbsp;
daughter in 1319 noted that the girl was “brown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:37.040
of skin all over, much like her father.” The&nbsp;
Bishop’s description of the Hainault girl has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:43.200
encouraged the notion that Philippa had African&nbsp;
ancestry, and she was controversially included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:51.040
in the 100 Great Black Britons project in&nbsp;
2003. While Philippa’s maternal ancestors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:57.760
included not only southern Europeans of darker&nbsp;
complexion but also a chieftain of the Cumans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:05.280
a nomadic Turkic people from central Asia, none&nbsp;
of this indicates African ancestry. It also does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:11.040
not account for the apparent dark skin of Count&nbsp;
William, who was exclusively of northern European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:17.440
descent. While it remains unclear what Stapleton&nbsp;
meant by describing the Count of Hainault and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:22.560
his daughter having brown skin all over,&nbsp;
Kathryn Warner observes that there is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:28.960
source during Philippa’s queenship that remarks&nbsp;
on her appearance being unconventional.   
&nbsp;

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:37.520
Philippa grew up at a time of great uncertainty&nbsp;
in western European politics. Medieval aristocrats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:42.800
travelled between their estates and Philippa’s&nbsp;
childhood was split between Valenciennes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:48.400
her father’s estates in Holland. Philippa&nbsp;
spoke French and Flemish and was likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:55.440
acquainted with the Arthurian romances and the&nbsp;
religious texts in her father’s small library.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.440 --> 00:07:02.080
In 1323, Count William made peace with the Count&nbsp;
of Flanders by giving up his claim to Flanders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:08.160
in exchange for formal recognition as Count of&nbsp;
Zeeland. This gave William the opportunity to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:14.080
arrange advantageous marriages for his daughters.&nbsp;
Philippa likely joined her family at the double&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:20.960
wedding ceremony in Cologne in February 1324,&nbsp;
where her elder sisters Margaret and Joanna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:29.200
were married to Ludwig IV of Bavaria and Wilhelm&nbsp;
of Jülich respectively. The 42-year-old Ludwig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:35.680
was one of Europe’s most powerful men. He had&nbsp;
recently defeated his cousin and rival, Frederick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:43.040
of Austria, consolidating his claim as King of&nbsp;
Germany. Wilhelm of Jülich was also heir to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:49.680
county of Jülich, which straddled the present-day&nbsp;
borders of Germany and the Netherlands. Philippa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:55.360
may have attended the wedding of her mother’s&nbsp;
cousins King Charles IV of France and Joan of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:04.320
Evreux in Paris on 5 July 1324. The youngest of&nbsp;
King Philip IV’s three sons, Charles had come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:10.960
the throne after his elder brothers died without&nbsp;
heirs. He was therefore desperate for a son to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:17.600
secure his lineage. In December 1325, Philippa&nbsp;
accompanied her mother to Paris to visit her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:24.240
dying grandfather Charles of Valois. During her&nbsp;
stay at the French capital, Philippa likely met&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:30.720
Edward of Windsor, the heir to the English throne,&nbsp;
and his mother Queen Isabella, who was the sister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:36.640
of the King of France. Isabella had travelled to&nbsp;
France to broker a peace agreement between her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:43.120
husband and her brother but was in no hurry to&nbsp;
return home. She was appalled by the influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:50.800
of Edward II’s courtier and rumoured lover, Hugh&nbsp;
Despenser the Younger. Hugh Despenser used his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:57.200
favoured position to appropriate the lands of&nbsp;
leading aristocrats and even the queen herself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:03.520
In late 1325, Isabella issued an ultimatum&nbsp;
and declared that she would remain in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:12.560
France until Edward dismissed Despenser.
The young Philippa was to be a major player in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:18.640
the scandal that embarrassed the English court.&nbsp;
Prince Edward had accompanied his mother to France&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:24.720
in order to pay homage to the King of France for&nbsp;
the duchy of Aquitaine, which was now reduced to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:30.880
the region of Gascony. Although Edward was&nbsp;
already betrothed to a Spanish princess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:36.720
Isabella may have been in correspondence with&nbsp;
the Hainault court about the prospect of a union&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:42.960
between Edward and Philippa even before the latter&nbsp;
arrived in Paris with her mother. An unofficial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:49.520
arrangement appears to have been made in December&nbsp;
1325 when Joan and Philippa were in France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:55.200
suggesting that Philippa met her future husband&nbsp;
for the first time. The pair may have met on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.200 --> 00:10:02.960
a second occasion at Joan of Evreux’s coronation&nbsp;
as Queen of France in May 1326, though Philippa’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:09.760
attendance is uncertain. The marital negotiations&nbsp;
could not have taken place without the knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:16.480
of Philippa’s father Count William. It was he&nbsp;
alone who could provide the men and ships for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:23.920
invasion of England and he had been on poor terms&nbsp;
with King Edward since the early 1320s. Relations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:30.640
between the two rulers deteriorated over pirate&nbsp;
raids in the North Sea. In addition, the prospect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:35.840
of having the heir to the English throne as his&nbsp;
son-in-law was attractive enough to encourage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:43.040
him to join the rebellion against the anointed&nbsp;
sovereign of England. In late 1325, Isabella&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:49.120
gained another powerful ally in Roger Mortimer, an&nbsp;
English nobleman who had escaped from the Tower of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:56.000
London after an unsuccessful rebellion to remove&nbsp;
Hugh Despenser. Philippa may have met Mortimer in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:03.280
early 1326 while he was in Hainault to discuss&nbsp;
invasion plans with Count William. Isabella and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:10.480
Edward visited the Hainault court in the summer,&nbsp;
and on 27 August Edward and Philippa were formally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:16.880
betrothed in the town of Mons. Even before&nbsp;
receiving news of the unsanctioned engagement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:23.200
King Edward wrote to his son demanding he&nbsp;
return to England. If the 13-year-old prince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:30.480
had even wished to do so, he was an effective&nbsp;
prisoner of his mother and had little choice.
&nbsp;

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:40.320
On 24 September 1326, Isabella and Mortimer&nbsp;
launched their successful invasion of England.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:46.400
Exactly two months later Hugh Despenser was&nbsp;
executed in gruesome fashion. As the pair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:52.480
took effective control of the kingdom, the&nbsp;
fate of Edward II remained open to question.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:59.440
In January 1327, Edward was forced to abdicate&nbsp;
the throne in favour of his teenage son, who was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:07.920
crowned King Edward III on 1 February 1327. The&nbsp;
deposed monarch, known as Edward of Caernarvon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:14.160
after his birthplace in Wales, lived in relative&nbsp;
comfort at Berkeley Castle in Gloucestershire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:22.480
until his mysterious death in September 1327.&nbsp;
Edward III later announced that his father had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:28.560
been murdered, and the late king’s fondness&nbsp;
for male companionship inspired the apocryphal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:37.040
tale that a red-hot poker had caused his demise.&nbsp;
Meanwhile, Philippa spent much of 1327 preparing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:43.680
for her nuptials with the newly enthroned Edward&nbsp;
III. Since Edward and Philippa were cousins, their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:50.960
proposed marriage required a papal dispensation,&nbsp;
which was granted in August by Pope John XXII in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:58.800
Avignon. Philippa landed in Dover in December&nbsp;
1327, a few days before Edward II’s funeral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:07.360
at Gloucester. She and Edward III were married in&nbsp;
York on 25 January 1328, the first anniversary of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:13.920
the King’s accession to the throne a year earlier.&nbsp;
Philippa of Hainault, just shy of her 14th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:21.200
birthday, was now England’s queen consort. Despite&nbsp;
this, she had little influence in a court which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:27.280
remained under the control of her mother-in-law&nbsp;
Isabella and her chief counsellor, Roger Mortimer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:34.000
Although the new regime had eliminated the hated&nbsp;
Despenser, Mortimer and Isabella were no less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:41.200
corrupt. For Edward III, the companionship of his&nbsp;
young wife would have been a welcome respite from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:50.240
his overbearing mother and her presumed lover. 
Philippa’s wedding was but one of three major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:57.280
events in two weeks which involved the Hainault&nbsp;
family and its close relatives. On 17 January&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.280 --> 00:14:05.200
1328, Philippa’s brother-in-law Ludwig of Bavaria&nbsp;
was crowned Holy Roman Emperor in Rome. Since Pope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:11.920
John XXII in Avignon was Ludwig’s enemy and&nbsp;
had excommunicated him, Ludwig was crowned by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:19.440
a cardinal and set up an antipope of his own. A&nbsp;
week after the wedding, King Charles IV of France&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:28.560
died aged 33 on 1 February 1328, leaving behind&nbsp;
two daughters and a pregnant wife. If Joan of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:35.600
Evreux were to give birth to a boy, the baby would&nbsp;
become King of France at the moment of his birth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:41.840
In the event, Joan gave birth to a daughter on 1&nbsp;
April, and with the extinction of the male line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:48.800
of King Philip IV, the closest claimant through&nbsp;
the female line was King Edward III of England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:56.240
Philip’s grandson via Queen Isabella. Although&nbsp;
Edward had the strongest claim, French elites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.240 --> 00:15:02.320
could never countenance the prospect of the King&nbsp;
of England becoming their own sovereign. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.320 --> 00:15:11.040
they enthroned Philippa’s maternal uncle Philip of&nbsp;
Valois, who became King Philip VI on 1 April 1328&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:20.880
and was crowned in Reims on 29 May 1328. Isabella&nbsp;
was infuriated by Philip’s accession, but Edward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:28.160
had no choice but to pay fealty to Philip in&nbsp;
1329 to secure his French domains of Ponthieu and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:36.160
Aquitaine. Meanwhile, Philippa was only granted&nbsp;
a modest annual allowance in April 1329 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:45.040
remained under the shadow of her mother-in-law.
King Edward himself was desperate to exercise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:52.560
his royal power. In October 1328, Roger Mortimer&nbsp;
became earl of March, giving him control of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:58.960
borderlands of England and Wales. This encouraged&nbsp;
a rebellion by the king’s great-uncle Henry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.960 --> 00:16:04.960
earl of Lancaster, which was defeated&nbsp;
by Mortimer’s forces in January 1329.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:10.480
While the failure of Lancaster’s rebellion&nbsp;
temporarily strengthened Mortimer’s position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:17.680
Edward III was cultivating his own supporters&nbsp;
behind the scenes. Meanwhile, rumours abounded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.680 --> 00:16:25.440
that the former Edward II was still alive in&nbsp;
Corfe Castle in Dorset. This prompted an active&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:32.000
campaign to liberate him and restore him to his&nbsp;
throne. Amid these uncertainties for the young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:39.200
royal couple, Philippa became pregnant in autumn&nbsp;
1329. This forced Isabella to arrange Philippa’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:46.080
coronation as Queen of England on 18 February&nbsp;
1330 and to grant her the lordship of Glamorgan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:52.320
in South Wales, which had been confiscated from&nbsp;
Hugh Despenser’s widow. Unbeknownst to the royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:58.800
family, the King’s uncle Edmund, Earl of Kent, was&nbsp;
plotting with William Melton, Archbishop of York,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:05.840
to free Edward II and restore him to power.&nbsp;
Kent’s swift arrest and execution in March&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.840 --> 00:17:12.560
1330 is an indication that the former King was&nbsp;
still alive and remained a threat to the regime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:20.000
Mortimer and Isabella were present for the birth&nbsp;
of Philippa’s son Edward on 15 June 1330 at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:26.880
Woodstock Palace in Oxfordshire. While Edward is&nbsp;
known to history as the Black Prince, during his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:34.160
lifetime he was known as Edward of Woodstock. His&nbsp;
birth strengthened King Edward’s hand, and on 19&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:41.120
October 1330 the King and a group of loyal knights&nbsp;
broke into Isabella’s chamber at Nottingham Castle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:46.800
while she was meeting with Mortimer and their&nbsp;
allies. Mortimer was arrested and was taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:54.800
to the Tower of London before being executed for&nbsp;
regicide on 29 November 1330. Isabella was granted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.800 --> 00:18:02.160
an allowance of £3,000 a year and remained at&nbsp;
liberty while being kept at a distance from court.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:08.720
The young King was keen to bring harmony to his&nbsp;
realm and restored the estates of the Despensers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.720 --> 00:18:14.000
Henry of Lancaster, and the family of&nbsp;
the late earl of Kent. Decades later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:22.800
Edward would also rehabilitate the Mortimer family&nbsp;
and bring them back into royal favour. 
&nbsp;

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:28.480
With Edward now in control of his kingdom,&nbsp;
Philippa assumed a more prominent role at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:34.880
the glamorous court festivities. She was given&nbsp;
a host of estates which increased her income to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:41.760
£4,000 a year, although this was not enough to&nbsp;
meet her household expenses. While she received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:48.320
additional sums from Edward’s Exchequer, her debts&nbsp;
continued to pile ever higher. Notwithstanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:55.280
their cost, the young royal couple’s lavish court&nbsp;
entertainments inspired a sense of optimism after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.280 --> 00:19:01.920
a decade of disorder. King Edward maintained&nbsp;
close relations with his in-laws, and in early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.920 --> 00:19:08.560
1331 Count William of Hainault went to France&nbsp;
to mediate on disagreements between England&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:14.800
and France. A few months later, Philippa’s mother,&nbsp;
Joan of Valois, visited England with her daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:21.040
Joanna and her son-in-law Count Wilhelm of Jülich.&nbsp;
They likely attended the series of jousting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.040 --> 00:19:27.840
tournaments which were held in the summer of 1331.&nbsp;
In September, Philippa was attending a joust at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:34.320
Cheapside in London when the ladies’ grandstand&nbsp;
collapsed. While the Queen’s coronet was damaged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.320 --> 00:19:40.080
she persuaded her husband not to punish the&nbsp;
carpenters, and the tournament continued the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:47.520
following morning after overnight repairs. In&nbsp;
late spring 1332, Philippa gave birth to her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:53.520
second child, a daughter named Isabella. While&nbsp;
the royal couple was staying at Knaresborough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.520 --> 00:20:01.440
Castle in April 1333, Philippa obtained a pardon&nbsp;
for a pregnant woman named Agnes of Scarborough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.440 --> 00:20:07.440
who had been sentenced to hanging for theft.&nbsp;
Philippa would gain a reputation for interceding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:14.160
on behalf of pregnant or young women facing&nbsp;
execution. Around this time, Philippa began her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.160 --> 00:20:20.080
third pregnancy and spent her first trimester&nbsp;
at Bamburgh Castle while the King went to war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.080 --> 00:20:26.800
with King David II of Scotland, the infant son of&nbsp;
Robert the Bruce and Edward’s own brother-in-law.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:32.880
After recapturing the port of Berwick-upon-Tweed,&nbsp;
Edward defeated a Scottish army at the Battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:40.960
of Halidon Hill on 18 July 1333. This was an&nbsp;
early demonstration of his military prowess. 
&nbsp;

00:20:44.080 --> 00:20:51.680
In January 1334 Philippa gave birth to her second&nbsp;
daughter, whom she named Joan after her mother. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.680 --> 00:20:57.760
Edward continued his war in Scotland, Philippa’s&nbsp;
brother-in-law Count Wilhelm of Jülich joined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.760 --> 00:21:05.760
Edward’s campaign in July and August 1335. Edward&nbsp;
also received the support of Robert of Artois,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:12.160
who was married to the namesake half-sister of&nbsp;
Philippa’s mother Joan of Valois. Robert fled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:18.400
into exile after attempting to claim the county&nbsp;
of Artois by forging his father’s will. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:24.560
meantime, his wife Joan was imprisoned by her&nbsp;
half-brother King Philip of France. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.560 --> 00:21:30.160
Edward aimed to strengthen his dynastic links&nbsp;
to the continent by arranging the marriage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:37.760
his newborn daughter Joan with Frederick, the son&nbsp;
of Duke Otto of Austria. As the sister-in-law of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.760 --> 00:21:44.080
the Holy Roman Emperor, Philippa could influence&nbsp;
the German princes on the matter. The royal couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.080 --> 00:21:51.920
remained in northern England over Christmas 1335,&nbsp;
and Philippa became pregnant again in April 1336.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.920 --> 00:21:57.680
While Edward resumed his Scottish campaign in the&nbsp;
summer, Philippa presided over a royal council&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:05.440
on 2 September 1336 to consider the situation in&nbsp;
Scotland, where Edward was attempting to install&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:05.440 --> 00:22:11.920
the nobleman Edward Balliol on the Scottish&nbsp;
throne in return for cessions of land. During&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.920 --> 00:22:18.800
the campaign, King Edward’s only brother John of&nbsp;
Eltham, the earl of Cornwall, died of a fever at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:18.800 --> 00:22:26.080
the age of 20. The royal bereavements continued&nbsp;
into early 1337, after Philippa gave birth to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.080 --> 00:22:32.560
short-lived son named William. A few months later,&nbsp;
Philippa’s father Count William of Hainault died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:43.600
on 7 June 1337. He was succeeded by his only&nbsp;
surviving son, Count William II of Hainault. 
&nbsp;

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:50.960
The fateful year of 1337 also witnessed the start&nbsp;
of the conflict between England and France known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.960 --> 00:22:58.000
as the Hundred Years War. This began when Philip&nbsp;
VI confiscated the duchy of Aquitaine from Edward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.000 --> 00:23:04.960
III in response to the latter’s refusal to give&nbsp;
up the fugitive Robert of Artois. In response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.960 --> 00:23:12.000
Edward revived his claim to the whole of France&nbsp;
and declared war on Philip. Between July 1338&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:19.520
and November 1340, Edward was frequently on the&nbsp;
continent seeking allies. Philippa accompanied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.520 --> 00:23:25.200
her husband on these diplomatic missions as&nbsp;
personal companion, trusted political advisor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:31.920
and a conduit to her powerful relatives on the&nbsp;
continent. Not long after their arrival in Antwerp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.920 --> 00:23:39.040
in July 1338, the royal couple’s lodgings caught&nbsp;
fire and they had to escape in their nightclothes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:39.040 --> 00:23:44.400
Philippa remained in Antwerp to give birth&nbsp;
to her second surviving son in November,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:44.400 --> 00:23:50.640
who was given the unusual name of Lionel after&nbsp;
the Arthurian knight. During the latter stages of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:50.640 --> 00:23:56.800
the Queen’s pregnancy, Edward sailed up the Rhine&nbsp;
with his brother-in-law Wilhelm of Jülich, whom he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.800 --> 00:24:04.000
created earl of Cambridge. At the city of Cologne,&nbsp;
where Wilhelm’s younger brother was archbishop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:10.160
the King worshiped at the shrine of the Three&nbsp;
Kings in the city’s cathedral. In September,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.160 --> 00:24:16.800
Edward met with Ludwig of Bavaria and senior&nbsp;
imperial princes at Koblenz, where he obtained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.800 --> 00:24:23.280
an alliance against France and an appointment&nbsp;
as a vicar of the Holy Roman Emperor. During&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:23.280 --> 00:24:30.080
Edward’s stay in Koblenz, he met a man called&nbsp;
“William le Galeys” or William the Welshman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.080 --> 00:24:37.440
who claimed to be the king’s father. The so-called&nbsp;
Fieschi letter, written by a papal notary to King&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:37.440 --> 00:24:44.400
Edward III in around 1337, claimed that the&nbsp;
deposed Edward II had escaped from Berkeley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.400 --> 00:24:50.960
Castle. From there, he had fled to the continent&nbsp;
before establishing himself in northern Italy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.960 --> 00:24:56.080
Royal accounts suggest that this William&nbsp;
could have accompanied the King to Antwerp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:56.080 --> 00:25:03.200
where he may have met Philippa. After becoming&nbsp;
pregnant for the sixth time in late spring 1339,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:09.680
Philippa spent the winter at Louvain. There she&nbsp;
met her brother Count William II and her mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:09.680 --> 00:25:18.640
Joan, who had retired to Fontenelle abbey near&nbsp;
Valenciennes after the death of her husband.  
&nbsp;

00:25:18.640 --> 00:25:25.600
On 26 January 1340, the royal family arrived&nbsp;
in the Flemish town of Ghent, where Edward III&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:25.600 --> 00:25:33.360
was officially proclaimed King of France. On 6&nbsp;
March 1340, Philippa gave birth to her son John,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:33.360 --> 00:25:40.480
known to history as John of Gaunt. Three months&nbsp;
later, King Edward III won a great naval victory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.480 --> 00:25:48.000
over the French at the Battle of Sluys on 24&nbsp;
June 1340. In September, while the English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:54.000
were besieging the French at Tournai, Edward&nbsp;
met Joan of Valois, who successfully brokered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:54.000 --> 00:26:00.960
an Anglo-French truce. This enabled Edward and&nbsp;
Philippa to return to England in November 1340&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:08.560
during the early stages of the Queen’s seventh&nbsp;
pregnancy. In January 1341, Philippa’s chaplain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:08.560 --> 00:26:15.840
Robert de Eglesfield founded a new college at&nbsp;
the University of Oxford named Queen’s Hall in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:22.960
her honour, which survives as Queen’s College.&nbsp;
In addition to the Oxford college, Philippa also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:22.960 --> 00:26:31.600
patronised several other religious houses and&nbsp;
hospitals around the country. On 5 June 1341,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:31.600 --> 00:26:38.240
Philippa gave birth to her son Edmund at the royal&nbsp;
palace of Langley in Hertfordshire. The birth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.240 --> 00:26:45.280
three healthy sons in less than three years helped&nbsp;
Edward III secure his line of succession but would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:45.280 --> 00:26:50.880
become a source of instability to the kingdom&nbsp;
by the end of the century. A few weeks earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:50.880 --> 00:26:55.680
the two-year-old Lionel of Antwerp&nbsp;
was betrothed to the eight-year-old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:55.680 --> 00:27:03.920
Elizabeth de Burgh, who stood to inherit her&nbsp;
family’s vast estates in Ireland. 
&nbsp;

00:27:03.920 --> 00:27:10.800
In late 1341, King Edward returned to lead a&nbsp;
campaign in Scotland. According to the French&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:16.880
chronicler Jean le Bel, the King developed an&nbsp;
infatuation for the countess of Salisbury after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:16.880 --> 00:27:23.920
rescuing her from a besieged castle. When she&nbsp;
resisted his advances, he violently assaulted her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:23.920 --> 00:27:31.200
and left her unconscious. Most historians reject&nbsp;
le Bel’s account since the count of Salisbury,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:38.160
William Montacute, was one of the King’s closest&nbsp;
friends. Kathryn Warner notes that le Bel referred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:44.960
to the countess as Alice rather than Catherine,&nbsp;
and may have confused her for Alice of Norfolk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:44.960 --> 00:27:51.840
who was brutally beaten to death in 1351&nbsp;
by her husband Edward Montacute, William’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:51.840 --> 00:27:59.920
younger brother. After returning to England, in&nbsp;
January 1342 Edward granted his son John of Gaunt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:06.320
the earldom of Richmond in Yorkshire. This had&nbsp;
previously belonged to Duke John III of Brittany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:06.320 --> 00:28:13.200
whose death in April 1341 caused a succession&nbsp;
crisis in the duchy that would reignite the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:19.280
Anglo-French conflict. In February, Philippa&nbsp;
joined Edward at a great tournament to celebrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.280 --> 00:28:24.880
their son Lionel’s betrothal to Elizabeth de&nbsp;
Burgh. By then she was heavily pregnant with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.880 --> 00:28:31.200
her daughter Blanche, who died shortly after&nbsp;
her birth in March. In late spring and summer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:37.440
Philippa’s brother Count William II visited&nbsp;
England to participate in a series of tournaments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:44.880
hosted by the King. These included the joust in&nbsp;
August 1342 which brought together the cream of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:44.880 --> 00:28:51.040
English nobility, its purpose being to celebrate&nbsp;
the wedding of Lionel of Antwerp and Elizabeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:51.040 --> 00:28:56.320
de Burgh. Although both were still children,&nbsp;
the King may have decided to formalise the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:56.320 --> 00:29:02.320
union to protect Elizabeth from abduction,&nbsp;
a fate that several wealthy heiresses had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.320 --> 00:29:08.400
suffered. Philippa was granted custody of her&nbsp;
daughter-in-law’s estates in Ireland until she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:14.000
attained her majority. Elizabeth joined her&nbsp;
husband Lionel and the younger royal children&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:24.240
in the joint household attached to the Queen.
In September 1342, Edward III intervened in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.240 --> 00:29:29.840
War of the Breton succession by sending an army&nbsp;
to support the claim of John of Montfort. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:29.840 --> 00:29:36.400
Edward himself soon left to take command, his&nbsp;
efforts were unsuccessful and the renegade Robert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:36.400 --> 00:29:43.440
of Artois was killed in action in November. Edward&nbsp;
of Woodstock had been formally appointed regent in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:43.440 --> 00:29:48.560
his father’s absence, though Queen Philippa&nbsp;
was entrusted with ensuring that the King’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:48.560 --> 00:29:56.240
instructions to bury Robert at Blackfriars church&nbsp;
were carried out. In May 1343, after returning to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:56.240 --> 00:30:03.200
England, the King made his son Edward the Prince&nbsp;
of Wales. Edward III held another magnificent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:03.200 --> 00:30:10.800
tournament of 300 knights at Windsor in January&nbsp;
1344, though it was at this event that his close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:10.800 --> 00:30:17.440
friend, the Count of Salisbury, was mortally&nbsp;
injured. Around this time, Dowager Queen Isabella&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:17.440 --> 00:30:23.840
joined Edward and Philippa on a hunting trip, an&nbsp;
indication of the King’s closer relations with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:23.840 --> 00:30:30.640
mother. Meanwhile, Philippa became pregnant with&nbsp;
her daughter Mary who was born in October 1344&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:30.640 --> 00:30:38.000
at Bishop’s Waltham in Hampshire. In 1344, King&nbsp;
Edward made serious efforts to strengthen his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:38.000 --> 00:30:45.200
alliance with his cousin Duke Jan III of Brabant&nbsp;
by proposing the marriage of his eldest children&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:45.200 --> 00:30:52.400
to those of the Duke. These efforts were abandoned&nbsp;
after Edward failed to secure papal dispensation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:52.400 --> 00:31:00.320
for the union of two pairs of cousins. In June&nbsp;
1345, the King turned his attention to the Iberian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:00.320 --> 00:31:06.640
Peninsula and proposed marrying the five-year-old&nbsp;
John of Gaunt to Queen Maria of Castile’s younger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:06.640 --> 00:31:14.400
sister Eleanor of Portugal, then aged 17. The&nbsp;
following month, Edward proposed the union of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:23.200
eldest daughter Isabella of Woodstock and Infante&nbsp;
Pedro, the son of King Alfonso XI of Castile. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:23.200 --> 00:31:29.440
the negotiations carried on into November,&nbsp;
Edward III proposed the 15-year-old Prince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:29.440 --> 00:31:40.400
of Wales as a more suitable match for Eleanor.
On 26 September 1345, Philippa’s younger brother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:40.400 --> 00:31:45.920
Count William II of Hainault was killed&nbsp;
while fighting rebels in Friesland. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:45.920 --> 00:31:52.400
the 38-year-old William had two illegitimate&nbsp;
sons, he had no legitimate children with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:52.400 --> 00:31:59.840
wife Johanna of Brabant. Under English law, the&nbsp;
property of a deceased landowner without a son was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:59.840 --> 00:32:06.880
divided among surviving daughters. Since William&nbsp;
had four surviving sisters, Edward III claimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:06.880 --> 00:32:13.760
that Philippa had the right to inherit a quarter&nbsp;
of William’s lands. Under inheritance laws on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:13.760 --> 00:32:20.800
the continent, all of William’s possessions were&nbsp;
inherited by his eldest sister Empress Margaret.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:20.800 --> 00:32:27.360
The dispute over the Hainault inheritance led&nbsp;
to tensions between Edward and his ally Emperor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:27.360 --> 00:32:34.240
Ludwig. King Philip VI supported Margaret’s claim&nbsp;
on the grounds that the Hainault counties were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:34.240 --> 00:32:41.120
already part of the Holy Roman Empire, while the&nbsp;
alternative would strengthen his English enemy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:41.120 --> 00:32:48.880
The situation was further complicated in summer&nbsp;
1346 when five of the seven imperial electors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:48.880 --> 00:32:56.640
voted to depose Ludwig as Holy Roman Emperor and&nbsp;
elect Charles of Luxembourg instead. Weeks after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:56.640 --> 00:33:02.880
his election, Charles and his blind father King&nbsp;
John of Bohemia joined the French army as Philip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:02.880 --> 00:33:09.600
VI prepared to respond to devastating raids across&nbsp;
Normandy by Edward III and the Prince of Wales in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:09.600 --> 00:33:17.280
July. What ensued was a devastating victory of&nbsp;
English longbowmen against the French knights at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:17.280 --> 00:33:23.360
the Battle of Crécy. The battle resulted in&nbsp;
the deaths of thousands of French noblemen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:23.360 --> 00:33:29.440
including several of Queen Philippa’s kinsmen.&nbsp;
The heavily pregnant Queen was obliged to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:29.440 --> 00:33:38.400
remain in England and gave birth to her tenth&nbsp;
child, Margaret of Windsor, on 20 July 1346. 
&nbsp;

00:33:40.240 --> 00:33:46.480
While her husband and son were on the continent,&nbsp;
a Scottish army invaded northern England. Three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:46.480 --> 00:33:52.240
Flemish chroniclers describe Philippa raising&nbsp;
an army and riding out to defeat the Scots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:52.240 --> 00:33:59.360
at the Battle of Neville’s Cross on 17 October,&nbsp;
during which King David II of Scotland fell into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:59.360 --> 00:34:05.360
English captivity. The tale of Philippa leading&nbsp;
the army at Neville’s Cross was repeated by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:05.360 --> 00:34:13.440
Tudor historian Raphael Holinshed. It is also&nbsp;
the subject of a 1789 painting by Benjamin West,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:13.440 --> 00:34:19.600
commissioned by King George III for Windsor&nbsp;
Castle. While Kathryn Warner dismisses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:19.600 --> 00:34:25.840
Philippa’s presence at Neville’s Cross by citing&nbsp;
correspondence in her name from Edward’s siege&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:25.840 --> 00:34:35.040
camp at Calais on 21 September and 23 October&nbsp;
1346, Jordan Michelle Schoonover argues that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:35.040 --> 00:34:41.440
was not inconceivable that the Queen performed&nbsp;
this action. She could, he argues, have sailed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:41.440 --> 00:34:47.840
to Newcastle-upon-Tyne to supervise the war effort&nbsp;
against the Scots before returning to Calais after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:47.840 --> 00:34:54.080
the victory. While the seven-year-old Lionel of&nbsp;
Antwerp was officially named regent during Edward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:54.080 --> 00:35:00.960
III’s absence in France, Philippa would have been&nbsp;
in a position to act on his behalf. Around this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:00.960 --> 00:35:07.840
time, Philippa met her sister Margaret at Ypres to&nbsp;
mend their relations over the Hainault succession.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:07.840 --> 00:35:15.600
In March 1347, the English royal couple and their&nbsp;
daughter Isabella met Louis de Male who had become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:15.600 --> 00:35:21.600
Count of Flanders after his father’s death at&nbsp;
Crécy. While Louis agreed to marry Isabella&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:21.600 --> 00:35:28.000
and recognise Edward as king of France, he&nbsp;
fled to the French court soon thereafter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:28.000 --> 00:35:34.960
Calais’ surrender in August 1347 created the&nbsp;
conditions for Philippa’s famous intercession&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:34.960 --> 00:35:41.120
to spare the burghers of Calais, whose anguish&nbsp;
was memorably depicted by the sculptor Auguste&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:41.120 --> 00:35:48.320
Rodin in the late 19th century. However, Kathryn&nbsp;
Warner and Lisa Hilton highlight inconsistencies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:48.320 --> 00:35:54.480
in Froissart’s account, such as the fact that&nbsp;
Philippa could not have been heavily pregnant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:54.480 --> 00:36:00.800
as the chroniclers described. Even if Philippa’s&nbsp;
intercession did otherwise take place in public,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:00.800 --> 00:36:06.560
they argue that Edward had made up his mind&nbsp;
to spare the burghers. However, the Queen’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:06.560 --> 00:36:13.440
intercession allowed him to do so in an apparently&nbsp;
spontaneous gesture of clemency while maintaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:13.440 --> 00:36:24.480
a hard line against those who defied his will.
In October 1347, Philippa’s brother-in-law Ludwig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:24.480 --> 00:36:32.000
of Bavaria died at the age of 65. His death&nbsp;
secured the position of Charles of Luxembourg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:32.000 --> 00:36:39.600
though in January 1348 Edward III received four&nbsp;
votes from German electors who hoped to install&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:39.600 --> 00:36:48.240
him as an anti-king to Charles. After Edward&nbsp;
refused, Charles’ election was confirmed in 1349,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:48.240 --> 00:36:55.520
and in 1355 he was crowned as the Holy Roman&nbsp;
Emperor, Charles IV. As Philippa and Edward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:55.520 --> 00:37:03.040
returned to England in October 1347, the Queen&nbsp;
was pregnant for the eleventh time. A few weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:03.040 --> 00:37:09.760
before she gave birth to William of Windsor&nbsp;
in May 1348, the King formally instituted the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:09.760 --> 00:37:16.640
Order of the Garter on 23 April, St George’s&nbsp;
Day. Inspired by King Arthur’s round table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:16.640 --> 00:37:22.720
Edward named 24 of his closest companions as&nbsp;
knights of the Garter with himself and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:22.720 --> 00:37:28.480
Prince of Wales at the top of the list.&nbsp;
According to legend, Edward adopted the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:28.480 --> 00:37:34.160
garter after the Countess of Salisbury’s garter&nbsp;
slipped off her leg at a ball. The King picked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:34.160 --> 00:37:42.640
it up and proclaimed “Honi soit qui mal y pense”.&nbsp;
This, translated, means, “Shame on he who thinks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:42.640 --> 00:37:48.880
ill of it”. Historians believe that the tale&nbsp;
was invented in the 15th century after men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:48.880 --> 00:37:55.120
stopped wearing garters, and Lisa Hilton argues&nbsp;
that the order’s motto referred to his claim to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:55.120 --> 00:38:02.880
the French throne. The optimism of the English&nbsp;
court in early 1348 also reflected successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:02.880 --> 00:38:10.080
marriage negotiations with Castile. In 1347, the&nbsp;
Castilians had agreed in principle to the marriage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:10.080 --> 00:38:16.480
of Eleanor of Portugal to Edward of Woodstock,&nbsp;
but the English envoys were significantly delayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:16.480 --> 00:38:24.080
and upon their arrival, Eleanor had already&nbsp;
married King Pedro IV of Aragon. In late 1347,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:24.080 --> 00:38:30.960
Edward III succeeded in arranging the marriage&nbsp;
of his second daughter Joan to Infante Pedro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:30.960 --> 00:38:38.640
the only legitimate son of King Alfonso XI of&nbsp;
Castile. The 14-year-old Joan left England in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:38.640 --> 00:38:45.600
the summer of 1348, but fell ill and died&nbsp;
in Gascony. She was one of millions who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:45.600 --> 00:38:51.760
succumbed to the Black Death, the bubonic plague&nbsp;
that killed prince and pauper as it swept through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:51.760 --> 00:38:58.160
Europe. Around this time, Philippa and Edward&nbsp;
were also mourning the death of the newborn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:58.160 --> 00:39:05.120
William. Some sources indicate that the royal&nbsp;
couple also lost a son named Thomas of Windsor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:05.120 --> 00:39:11.760
to the plague. The historian Kathryn Warner,&nbsp;
however, argues that Thomas never existed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:11.760 --> 00:39:20.240
was invented to reconcile the Flemish accounts&nbsp;
of Philippa being heavily pregnant at Calais.  
&nbsp;

00:39:20.240 --> 00:39:27.440
In late February 1350, Alfonso XI of Spain&nbsp;
succumbed to the plague and was succeeded by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.440 --> 00:39:34.880
Pedro, who imprisoned and executed his father’s&nbsp;
lover Eleanor de Guzmán. This provoked a civil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:34.880 --> 00:39:41.920
war between King Pedro and his illegitimate&nbsp;
half-brother Enrique de Trastámara, who eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:41.920 --> 00:39:50.160
emerged victorious in 1369. Meanwhile, King&nbsp;
Edward was irritated by pirate attacks on English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:50.160 --> 00:39:58.080
shipping led by the Franco-Castilian nobleman&nbsp;
Carlos de la Cerda. On 29 August 1350, King&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:58.080 --> 00:40:04.800
Edward III intercepted and defeated the Castilian&nbsp;
fleet at the Battle of Winchelsea off the Sussex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:04.800 --> 00:40:12.240
coast. Large numbers of enemy ships were captured&nbsp;
and sunk, although the English lost significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:12.240 --> 00:40:18.560
numbers of men. The Prince of Wales and John&nbsp;
of Gaunt both participated in the battle, while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:18.560 --> 00:40:25.600
Queen Philippa anxiously observed proceedings from&nbsp;
shore. A few days earlier, Philippa’s uncle King&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:25.600 --> 00:40:33.520
Philip VI of France died and was succeeded by his&nbsp;
son King John II. Her mother Joan of Valois died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:33.520 --> 00:40:41.200
less than two years later on 7 March 1352 in her&nbsp;
late fifties. Philippa herself was in her late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:41.200 --> 00:40:47.840
thirties and had been Queen of England for over&nbsp;
two decades. The Queen had not been pregnant since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:47.840 --> 00:40:56.240
the birth of the short-lived William of Windsor&nbsp;
in 1348, but on 7 January 1355 she gave birth to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:56.240 --> 00:41:03.520
a son named Thomas at Woodstock. Later that year,&nbsp;
Lionel of Antwerp and Elizabeth de Burgh gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:03.520 --> 00:41:14.480
Philippa her first legitimate grandchild in August&nbsp;
1355, a girl named Philippa, after the Queen. 
&nbsp;

00:41:14.480 --> 00:41:20.800
Meanwhile, the war against France gained&nbsp;
renewed intensity, and on 19 September&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:20.800 --> 00:41:27.360
1356 the Black Prince won a great victory at&nbsp;
the Battle of Poitiers. Philippa’s cousin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:27.360 --> 00:41:34.960
King John II of France, was captured and paraded&nbsp;
through the streets of London in May 1357.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:34.960 --> 00:41:41.600
The imprisoned King spent the remainder of his&nbsp;
life in relative comfort at Savoy Palace in London&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:41.600 --> 00:41:48.000
and attended the celebrations of the Order of the&nbsp;
Garter in April 1358, when Philippa was appointed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:48.000 --> 00:41:54.240
the first Lady of the Garter. The event was&nbsp;
the last major public appearance of the Dowager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:54.240 --> 00:42:01.680
Queen Isabella, who died in August in her early&nbsp;
sixties. Not long afterwards, Philippa injured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:01.680 --> 00:42:07.920
her shoulder after falling from her horse while&nbsp;
hunting. In November Philippa and Edward attended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:07.920 --> 00:42:14.000
the wedding of their three-year-old granddaughter&nbsp;
Philippa with Edmund Mortimer. He was the son and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:14.000 --> 00:42:20.080
heir of Roger Mortimer, a knight of the Garter and&nbsp;
the namesake grandson of the man who had deposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:20.080 --> 00:42:26.720
Edward II. The royal couple hurried to make&nbsp;
arrangements for their seven unmarried children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:26.720 --> 00:42:35.040
and on 19 May 1359, John of Gaunt married Blanche&nbsp;
of Lancaster, whose father Henry of Grosmont was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:35.040 --> 00:42:41.200
the Duke of Lancaster and one of Edward’s most&nbsp;
accomplished military leaders. The same day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:41.200 --> 00:42:47.520
the Queen’s youngest daughter Margaret of Windsor&nbsp;
married John Hastings, heir to the earldom of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:47.520 --> 00:42:54.160
Pembroke in South Wales and a maternal grandson&nbsp;
of the first Roger Mortimer. When King Edward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:54.160 --> 00:43:00.480
and his four eldest sons returned to campaign in&nbsp;
France in October, the Queen remained in England&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:00.480 --> 00:43:08.080
with the pregnant Blanche of Lancaster, who gave&nbsp;
birth to a daughter on 31 March 1360. The girl was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:08.080 --> 00:43:14.720
named Philippa after her grandmother and would&nbsp;
later become Queen of Portugal. After Edward’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:14.720 --> 00:43:20.960
return to England in May 1360, he combined&nbsp;
his household with the Queen’s in an effort to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:20.960 --> 00:43:30.480
take control of Philippa’s spiralling debts. 
The royal marriages continued in spring 1361,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:30.480 --> 00:43:36.960
when the Prince of Wales married his recently&nbsp;
widowed cousin Joan of Kent. Since Joan was in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:36.960 --> 00:43:43.280
her thirties and had previously been bigamously&nbsp;
married to Thomas Holland and William Montacute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:43.280 --> 00:43:50.000
2nd Earl of Salisbury, Philippa and Edward were&nbsp;
initially opposed to the marriage. However, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:50.000 --> 00:43:57.920
gave their consent in the autumn. In the spring of&nbsp;
1361, Philippa’s daughter Mary of Waltham married&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:57.920 --> 00:44:03.680
John de Montfort, the claimant to the duchy of&nbsp;
Brittany. While John would later take control of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:03.680 --> 00:44:10.320
Brittany, the 17-year-old Mary died in September&nbsp;
a few months after the wedding. Her younger sister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:10.320 --> 00:44:16.480
Margaret followed her to the grave a few weeks&nbsp;
later, leaving Isabella of Woodstock as Philippa’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:16.480 --> 00:44:24.640
only surviving daughter. In June 1362, the Black&nbsp;
Prince was named prince of Aquitaine and crossed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:24.640 --> 00:44:31.040
the Channel to take over its government, never&nbsp;
to see his mother again. To mark his fiftieth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:31.040 --> 00:44:38.880
birthday in November 1362, the King named his&nbsp;
sons Lionel of Antwerp, John of Gaunt, and Edmund&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:38.880 --> 00:44:46.080
of Langley the dukes of Clarence, Lancaster, and&nbsp;
Cambridge respectively. Although Philippa was not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:46.080 --> 00:44:53.680
yet 50, her health deteriorated rapidly after her&nbsp;
shoulder injury. While Edward III is unlikely to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:53.680 --> 00:44:59.440
have remained entirely faithful to Philippa during&nbsp;
their first three decades of marriage, there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:59.440 --> 00:45:06.480
no record of the King fathering any illegitimate&nbsp;
children. The Queen’s illness led Edward to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:06.480 --> 00:45:14.560
on Alice Perrers from the Queen’s household as his&nbsp;
permanent mistress. Alice, who was born in 1348,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:14.560 --> 00:45:23.840
gave Edward a son and two daughters between&nbsp;
1364 and 1366. Given her own incapacity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:23.840 --> 00:45:29.680
Philippa may have given her blessing to the King’s&nbsp;
affair. Royal records show that the Queen was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:29.680 --> 00:45:38.320
still able to travel, albeit less frequently. In&nbsp;
January 1365, Philippa celebrated the news of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:38.320 --> 00:45:46.320
birth of Edward of Angouléme, the first son of the&nbsp;
Black Prince and Joan of Kent. Six months later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:46.320 --> 00:45:53.520
Philippa’s 33-year-old daughter Isabella of&nbsp;
Woodstock married Enguerrand de Coucy. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:53.520 --> 00:46:01.200
a French nobleman up to ten years her junior who&nbsp;
was made Earl of Bedford in 1366.          
&nbsp;

00:46:02.960 --> 00:46:10.160
In January 1367, Philippa received news&nbsp;
that Edward and Joan had their second son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:10.160 --> 00:46:16.560
named Richard of Bordeaux. Three months later,&nbsp;
John of Gaunt and Blanche of Lancaster had their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:16.560 --> 00:46:23.280
first son, Henry Bolingbroke. Philippa could not&nbsp;
have known at the time that the two boys would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:23.280 --> 00:46:30.720
become England’s next two kings, Richard II and&nbsp;
Henry IV. At the time of his son’s birth, John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:30.720 --> 00:46:37.040
of Gaunt was campaigning in Spain with his brother&nbsp;
Edward. Although the campaign had been successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:37.040 --> 00:46:45.520
the Black Prince fell seriously ill and remained&nbsp;
an invalid until his death in 1376. Queen Philippa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:45.520 --> 00:46:52.400
herself was approaching her end, but she lived to&nbsp;
learn of the demise of her 30-year-old son Lionel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:52.400 --> 00:47:00.080
of Antwerp, who died in Italy in October 1368&nbsp;
shortly after his marriage to his second wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:00.080 --> 00:47:07.040
Violante Visconti. The previous month, Philippa’s&nbsp;
daughter-in-law Blanche of Lancaster died at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:07.040 --> 00:47:14.000
age of 26. In December, Philippa attempted to&nbsp;
broker the marriage of the widowed John of Gaunt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:14.000 --> 00:47:20.400
with Margaret of Flanders, the heiress to Flanders&nbsp;
and four French counties. She was too late,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:20.400 --> 00:47:26.320
as Count Louis of Flanders informed her that&nbsp;
Margaret was to marry Philip, the youngest brother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:26.320 --> 00:47:33.760
of King Charles V of France. King Charles was also&nbsp;
the Duke of Burgundy and the founder of one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:33.760 --> 00:47:40.240
most powerful dynasties of late medieval Europe.&nbsp;
The negotiations show that Philippa remained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:40.240 --> 00:47:48.960
politically active despite her poor health. On 22&nbsp;
June 1369, Philippa interceded on behalf of Alice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:48.960 --> 00:47:55.440
Marchant and Margaret Melbury, two pregnant&nbsp;
women who faced the death penalty for theft.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:55.440 --> 00:48:03.920
These were among her last items of business,&nbsp;
and she died on 15 August 1369, aged 55,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:03.920 --> 00:48:12.880
with her husband King Edward III and her youngest&nbsp;
son Thomas of Woodstock in attendance.      
&nbsp;

00:48:12.880 --> 00:48:17.440
Philippa of Hainault was one of the&nbsp;
longest-serving queen consorts in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:17.440 --> 00:48:23.040
English history. Born into a small but&nbsp;
ambitious dynasty in the Low Countries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:23.040 --> 00:48:29.200
Philippa was not only Queen of England but closely&nbsp;
related to the king of France and the Holy Roman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:29.200 --> 00:48:36.320
Emperor. In nearly four decades as Edward III’s&nbsp;
queen, she was almost always at her husband’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:36.320 --> 00:48:44.000
side at home and abroad as a trusted advisor and&nbsp;
diplomat. While she struggled to keep control of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:44.000 --> 00:48:50.240
expenditures at her husband’s glamorous court,&nbsp;
the Flemish chroniclers show her as an effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:50.240 --> 00:48:56.880
deputy for the King in political and military&nbsp;
affairs. Philippa’s tenure as queen witnessed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:56.880 --> 00:49:03.440
early decades of the Hundred Years War, including&nbsp;
the great English victories at Crécy and Poitiers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:03.440 --> 00:49:10.800
as well as the Black Death and further outbreaks&nbsp;
of plague. In an era of high mortality, Philippa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:10.800 --> 00:49:17.920
gave her husband twelve children and outlived&nbsp;
seven of them. While none of her sons ever became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:17.920 --> 00:49:27.360
King, her grandson Richard II succeeded to the&nbsp;
throne in June 1377 after the death of Edward III.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:27.360 --> 00:49:33.280
The Wars of the Roses that tore England&nbsp;
apart in the 15th century would be fought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:33.280 --> 00:49:42.240
between the descendants of her third son, John&nbsp;
of Gaunt and her fourth son, Edmund of Langley.
&nbsp;

00:49:42.240 --> 00:49:48.480
What do you think of Philippa of Hainault?&nbsp;
Was she one of the most effective queens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:48.480 --> 00:49:55.520
of medieval England, or was her influence&nbsp;
exaggerated by chroniclers close to her court?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:55.520 --> 00:50:19.360
Please let us know in the comment section and in&nbsp;
the meantime, thank you very much for watching.

